exec(open('patch1.py').read().split("sub('w1.py'")[0])
sub('w1.py',[
("Who sends the property tax bill in Wilson County?","Who collects property tax in Wilson County?"),
("The office is at 113 Nash Street East in the city of Wilson and keeps weekday hours from 8:00 am to 5:00 pm. Its page lists four ways to pay, including in person and online.","Its counter sits at 113 Nash Street East in the city of Wilson, and its page lists four ways to pay, online among them."),
("What does Wilson County look like for a seller?","What should a Wilson County seller know about the local housing stock?"),
("shows these figures for the county:","reports these county figures:"),
("81,150 residents in the July 2025 estimate, a 2.4% rise since the 2020 count.","A 2025 estimate of 81,150 people, a 2.4% rise since the 2020 count."),
("38,463 housing units, with a 60.0% owner-occupied rate for 2020 to 2024.","38,463 housing units, with owners living in 60.0% of occupied homes."),
("A median owner-occupied value of $186,000 and a median gross rent of $953.","Typical owned homes valued at $186,000 and typical rent of $953."),
("Median household income of $56,423 and a 22.5 minute mean commute.","Middle household income of $56,423 and a 22.5 minute average commute."),
("A seller here is working with a market where six in ten homes are owner-occupied, so many buyers are local households rather than investors.","With six in ten homes lived in by their owners, many local sellers are also long-time residents."),
("The county's FAQ explains a point many sellers miss: value, ownership and location are fixed as of January 1, so the bill goes to whoever owned the parcel that day. A sale later in the year does not move that bill, which is why closing statements prorate it.","The county's FAQ answers a common seller question. Whoever owned the parcel on the first day of the year receives the bill, and a later sale usually changes the name only for the following year."),
("Ask the tax office for the current appeal form and deadline. The county's 2024 real property deadline closed on May 16, 2024, when the Board of Equalization and Review adjourned, and the next window opened in January 2025 for the 2025 tax year.","Get the current form and cutoff from the tax office. After the 2024 revaluation, the real property window closed May 16, 2024, when the review board adjourned, and a new one opened in January 2025."),
("The unpaid tax stays attached to the parcel, so it has to be settled at or before closing. A tax lien attaches as of the January 1 listing date, per <a href=\"@S@GS_105-355.html\">G.S. 105-355</a>, and the county can enforce it by a foreclosure action under G.S. 105-374.","A balance follows the parcel into the sale, so closing is the moment to clear it. The lien arises on the listing date each year (<a href=\"@S@GS_105-355.html\">G.S. 105-355</a>), and collection can end in a court-supervised sale."),
("Where do I pay Wilson County property tax?","What are the ways to pay Wilson County tax?"),
("Pay at the tax office at 113 Nash Street East in Wilson on weekdays from 8:00 am to 5:00 pm, or use the county's online payment site. The tax administration page lists four payment methods, so check it for the one that suits you.","Visit the counter at 113 Nash Street East in Wilson, or use the county's online payment site. The tax administration page lists four methods in all."),
("Who gets the tax bill if I sell after January 1?","Who receives the bill if I sell during the year?"),
("The owner of record on January 1 gets the bill. Wilson County's FAQ says a sale later in the year normally shows up on the next year's bill, so the buyer and seller settle the share at closing.","The January 1 owner. The county's FAQ says a mid-year sale normally shows up the next tax year, so buyer and seller settle the split at closing."),
("Is the last day to pay in Wilson County January 5 or January 6?","Is the Wilson County cutoff January 5 or January 6?"),
("Can I still appeal my Wilson County value?","Is a Wilson County appeal still open to me?"),
])
sub('w2.py',[
("Which fees apply at the Wilson County recording counter?","What are the recording charges in Wilson County?"),
("Who pays the transfer tax on a Wilson County deed?","Which side pays the Wilson County deed stamp tax?"),
("The seller pays it. North Carolina's excise tax on conveyances is one dollar on each $500, or fraction of $500, of the consideration or value, and","The seller does. The state levy is $1.00 per $500 (or part of $500) of value conveyed, and"),
("says the transferor pays the register before the deed is recorded.","puts the payment on the transferor, due to the register before filing."),
("Half the money stays in the county general fund, and the other half goes monthly to the state Department of Revenue. In practice a closing attorney totals the tax for the contract price and collects it from the seller's proceeds. Ask the attorney for the figure instead of estimating, since the rounding rule on partial $500 amounts changes the result.","The county keeps one half and the state gets the other half monthly. The closing attorney totals the tax and takes it from the seller's proceeds, so ask for the exact figure."),
("Where do I record a deed for a Wilson County house?","Where is a Wilson County deed filed?"),
("Record it with the Wilson County Register of Deeds, and call 252-399-2935 for the current process. Closing attorneys usually handle the filing for a sale.","With the county Register of Deeds, reachable at 252-399-2935. For a sale, the closing attorney usually files it."),
("How much does it cost to record a deed in Wilson County?","What fee applies to a general deed in Wilson County?"),
("A general instrument costs $26.00 for the first 15 pages and $4.00 for each additional page under G.S. 161-10. A deed of trust costs $64.00 for the first 35 pages.","G.S. 161-10 sets $26.00 for 15 pages, then $4.00 per extra page. Deeds of trust run $64.00 for 35 pages."),
("Who pays the excise tax when a house is sold in Wilson County?","Who owes the excise tax on a Wilson County sale?"),
("The seller pays it to the register before recording, at one dollar per $500 of the price or fraction of that amount.","The seller, paid to the register before recording."),
])
sub('w3.py',[
("Winston-Salem is the largest city in Forsyth County, with a July 2025 estimate of 257,271 residents, according to Census","The city counted 257,271 people in its 2025 estimate, per Census"),
("Forsyth County Tax Administration is the office to call. Winston-Salem sits in Forsyth County, and the county's tax office takes real estate appeals, relief applications and ownership questions. It is in the Forsyth County Government Center at 201 N. Chestnut Street, and its phone number is 336-703-2300.","Winston-Salem property owners deal with Forsyth County Tax Administration, which takes appeals, relief applications and ownership questions. Visit the Forsyth County Government Center at 201 N. Chestnut Street or call 336-703-2300."),
])
